WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.531
Picture the busiest branch of our post office.

2
00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:09.778
At the door, a doorman lets customers in and shows each one to a box.

3
00:00:09.778 --> 00:00:13.532
Two light-watchers each mind a section of the wall of lights.

4
00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:16.487
And every one of them comes from one staff room.

5
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.530
Now a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:23.150
Say my handler is slow: a 300 millisecond price lookup.

7
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.178
When its light comes on, who actually runs it?

8
00:00:26.178 --> 00:00:29.337
The watcher who saw the light, or somebody else?

9
00:00:29.337 --> 00:00:31.048
Make a guess, and keep it.

10
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.305
You've met two answers already.

11
00:00:36.305 --> 00:00:40.115
Netty's clerk serves every job himself, on the spot.

12
00:00:40.115 --> 00:00:45.831
Tomcat, another Java web server, hands every request to a pool of 200 threads.

13
00:00:45.831 --> 00:00:50.593
Jetty 12, one of the oldest Java web servers, decides job by job.

14
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.294
First, the staff.

15
00:00:52.294 --> 00:00:57.316
The doorman is an acceptor: it calls accept, and hands each new connection on.

16
00:00:57.316 --> 00:01:02.466
A light-watcher is a selector: one thread running the select loop from lesson 2.

17
00:01:02.466 --> 00:01:06.587
And the handler is your code, here PriceHandler, the price desk.

18
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.909
The lesson's demo counted a default server on 4 CPUs: one acceptor and two selectors.

19
00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:22.899
The staff room is a QueuedThreadPool, with 8 to 200 threads, and the connector leases 3 of them for good: the doorman and the two watchers.

20
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.659
Now a light comes on.

21
00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:32.001
The watcher makes a task: run this connection, which means, read the request and call the handler.

22
00:01:32.001 --> 00:01:33.101
Who should do it?

23
00:01:33.101 --> 00:01:36.337
There are three choices, and each one has a price.

24
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:37.670
One detail first.

25
00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.536
The watcher has just read this customer's form, so it's still in his hands.

26
00:01:42.536 --> 00:01:49.155
Inside a computer, that's the CPU's cache, its small fast memory, still holding the connection's data.

27
00:01:49.155 --> 00:01:52.529
Anyone else has to fetch the form and read it again.

28
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:56.549
Choice one: the watcher serves the job himself, on the spot.

29
00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.583
No hand-off, form still in hand.

30
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:02.780
But while he serves, his whole section of the wall goes unwatched.

31
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:08.312
Jetty calls this Produce-Consume, and its docs say it's the same as a Netty event loop.

32
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:14.731
Choice two: the watcher puts the job in the staff room's queue, and a pool clerk takes it.

33
00:02:14.731 --> 00:02:16.083
No section goes dark.

34
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:21.043
But there's a hand-off, a queue, and a clerk who reads the form from scratch.

35
00:02:21.043 --> 00:02:23.040
That's Produce-Execute-Consume.

36
00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:25.558
Choice three, the clever one.

37
00:02:25.558 --> 00:02:30.190
The staff room keeps a standby clerk idle and ready: a reserved thread.

38
00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:36.843
The watcher waves the standby over to mind the lights, and serves the job himself, form still in hand.

39
00:02:36.843 --> 00:02:39.257
Warm cache, and no section goes dark.

40
00:02:39.257 --> 00:02:41.279
That's Execute-Produce-Consume.

41
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.964
So how does Jetty choose?

42
00:02:43.964 --> 00:02:48.215
It reads the label on the job: your handler's superclass.

43
00:02:48.215 --> 00:02:50.825
Handler.Abstract says: I may block.

44
00:02:50.825 --> 00:02:54.479
Handler.Abstract.NonBlocking says: I never block.

45
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.108
A non-blocking task runs on the spot.

46
00:02:57.108 --> 00:03:03.225
A blocking task gets a standby, if a reserved thread is free, and goes to the pool if none is.

47
00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:09.554
Jetty's source says this strategy used to be called Eat What You Kill: the hunter eats what it caught.

48
00:03:09.554 --> 00:03:15.735
Our post office would put it more gently: the one who spots the job does the job, whenever that's safe.

49
00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:19.380
Let's test it with the lesson's StrategyDemo.

50
00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.381
It reads the mode from each handler's own stack.

51
00:03:22.381 --> 00:03:25.632
A blocking handler, on the default pool: which mode?

52
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.829
epcRunTask: Execute-Produce-Consume.

53
00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:37.221
The watcher who saw the light ran the slow job himself, while a standby took over the wall.

54
00:03:37.221 --> 00:03:39.328
That answers our shy question.

55
00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:43.545
The non-blocking handler: pcRunTask, served on the spot.

56
00:03:43.545 --> 00:03:47.844
Now set the reserved threads to zero, so there's no standby.

57
00:03:47.844 --> 00:03:48.919
Which mode now?

58
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.322
No strategy frame at all.

59
00:03:53.322 --> 00:03:57.178
The job was handed to the pool: Produce-Execute-Consume.

60
00:03:57.178 --> 00:04:03.306
And with a virtual threads executor, the hand-off goes to a temporary helper, jetty-vt-1.

61
00:04:03.306 --> 00:04:05.579
That's the lesson's next section.

62
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.238
Now the trap.

63
00:04:07.238 --> 00:04:12.010
Someone marks the slow price desk NonBlocking, because that sounds faster.

64
00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:18.652
The watcher serves the 300 millisecond lookup on the spot, and his whole section of the wall goes dark.

65
00:04:18.652 --> 00:04:21.360
The docs call it a risk of server lock-up.

66
00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:24.134
It's the express lane at the supermarket.

67
00:04:24.134 --> 00:04:26.262
Ten items or fewer, says the sign.

68
00:04:26.262 --> 00:04:29.706
Bring a full cart, and the whole lane waits behind you.

69
00:04:29.706 --> 00:04:32.836
The label is a promise, and Jetty plans around it.

70
00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:35.004
And here the picture breaks.

71
00:04:35.004 --> 00:04:38.949
A real watcher might notice a long job and call for help.

72
00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.264
Jetty never checks.

73
00:04:40.264 --> 00:04:41.648
It trusts the label.

74
00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:43.335
One more rule.

75
00:04:43.335 --> 00:04:48.731
Handlers can hold other handlers, and one blocking child makes the whole tree blocking.

76
00:04:48.731 --> 00:04:51.212
That's safe, but it costs the fast path.

77
00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:55.355
Here are the two labels in code, Java and Kotlin.

78
00:04:55.355 --> 00:04:59.084
PriceHandler may block, so it extends Handler.Abstract.

79
00:04:59.084 --> 00:05:04.576
StatusHandler only writes one short line, so it can honestly promise NonBlocking.

80
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.334
So, who runs my slow handler?

81
00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:14.668
Usually the watcher who saw the light, with a standby minding the wall, or a pool clerk if no standby is free.

82
00:05:14.668 --> 00:05:16.469
Jetty picks from the label.

83
00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:19.869
Declare NonBlocking only for code that never waits.

84
00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:20.957
Quick check.

85
00:05:20.957 --> 00:05:26.388
The pool has no reserved threads, and a non-blocking handler gets a request.

86
00:05:26.388 --> 00:05:27.245
Who runs it?

87
00:05:29.933 --> 00:05:31.690
The watcher, on the spot.

88
00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:35.835
A non-blocking task never needs a standby: Produce-Consume.
